**Read:** [Nonvolatile residue collection and analysis — Gordon, Rutherford and Aranda](../sources/Nonvolatile-Residue-Collection-and-Analysis-NASA.lingua#r_2YMCRSWR07NCHECM0X4D8FJ6HJ), FTIR and GCMS discussion and the relevant instrument appendix; [Cleaning and surface properties — Mauro Taborelli](../sources/Cleaning-and-Surface-Properties-Taborelli.lingua#r_1RDARBEFXDAW9Y2DNTJ2QBEP2E), section 3.1.

Gravimetry estimates an amount under its preparation method. Infrared features can support functional-group assignments; chromatographic retention and mass spectra can distinguish components under the method's separation and detection limits. None supplies a universal identity or concentration without references, blanks and an observation model.

A library match is a candidate interpretation. Co-elution, mixtures, matrix effects, preparation losses and instrument response can produce ambiguity. An extract averages its sampled area, while a surface-sensitive probe observes a particular location and depth. Comparing their numbers requires those different sampling bases.

**Work:** Design an evidence chain for an unknown film using one amount measurement and two complementary identification observations. Distinguish confirmed components from tentative assignments. Explain why an absent peak is a method-dependent limit, and choose reference material or an independent observation that could challenge your interpretation. Deeper instrument physics follows in the measurement formation.

**Return:** [Clean manufacturing and contamination control — from familiar objects to controlled processes](../Clean-manufacturing-and-contamination-control.lingua#r_7D8SQ8BVQDZQBDBGC6DP0JAMZN).
